WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.110
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:09.490
A few years ago, we have mainly seen neural
network-based techniques being used for image

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.790
classification.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:15.660
This means that they were able to recognize
objects, for instance, animals and traffic

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:16.960
signs in images.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.180
But today, with the incredible pace of machine
learning research, we now have a selection

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:28.090
of neural network-based techniques for not
only classifying images, but, also, synthesizing

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.090
them!

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:34.090
The images that you see here and throughout
this video is generated by one of these learning-based

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.090
methods.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:40.850
But of course, in this series, we are always
obsessed with artistic control, or, in other

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.379
words, how much of a say we have in the creation
of these images.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:50.370
After all, getting thousands and thousands
of images without any overarching theme or

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.160
artistic control is hardly useful for anyone.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:59.460
One way of being able to control the outputs
is to use a technique that is capable of image

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.680
translation.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.360
What you see here is a work by the name CycleGAN!

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.439
It could transform apples into oranges, zebras
into horses, and more.

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:14.640
It was called CycleGAN because it introduced
a cycle consistency loss function.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.119
This means that if we convert a summer image
to a winter image, and then back to a summer

00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:25.860
image, we should get the same input image
back, or at least, something very similar.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.709
If our learning system obeys this principle,
the output quality of the translation is going

00:01:30.709 --> 00:01:32.770
to be significantly better.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:37.590
Today, we are going to study a more advanced
image translation technique that takes this

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:38.590
further.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:42.180
This paper is amazingly good at daytime image
translation.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:48.429
It looks at a selection of landscape images,
and then, as you see here, it learns to reimagine

00:01:48.429 --> 00:01:53.039
our input photos as if they were taken at
different times of the day.

00:01:53.039 --> 00:01:58.749
I love how clouds form, and move over time
in the synthesized images, and the night sky

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:02.399
with the stars is also truly a sight to behold.

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:08.819
But wait, CycleGAN and many other followup
works did image translation, this also does

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:12.520
image translation, so, what’s really new
here?

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:19.129
Well, one, this work proposes a novel upsampling
scheme that helps creating output images with

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:21.170
lots and lots of detail.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:27.269
Two, it can also create not just a bunch of
images a few hours apart, but it can also

00:02:27.269 --> 00:02:31.659
make beautiful timelapse videos, where the
transitions are smooth.

00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:33.269
Oh my goodness.

00:02:33.269 --> 00:02:34.989
I love this.

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:40.019
And three, the training happens by shoveling
20 thousand landscape images into the neural

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:45.599
network, and it becomes able to perform this
translation task without labels.

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:49.900
This means that we don’t have to explicitly
search for all the daytime images and tell

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:54.939
the learner that these are daytime images,
and these other images are not.

00:02:54.939 --> 00:03:00.540
This is amazing because the algorithm is able
to learn by itself, without labels, but it

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.489
is also easier to use because we can feed
in lots and lots more training data without

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:08.189
having to label these images correctly.

00:03:08.189 --> 00:03:14.079
As a result, we now know that this daytime
translation task is used as a testbed to demonstrate

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:19.250
that this method can be reused for other kinds
of image translation tasks.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.459
The fact that it can learn on its own and
still compete with other works in this area

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:26.209
is truly incredible.

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:31.810
Due to this kind of generality, it can also
perform other related tasks, for instance,

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:38.109
it can perform style transfer, or in other
words, not just change the time of day, but

00:03:38.109 --> 00:03:41.599
reimagine our pictures in the style of famous
artists.

00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:46.480
I think with this paper, we have a really
capable technique on our hands that is getting

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.819
closer and closer to the point where they
can see use in mainstream software packages

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:53.549
and image editors.

00:03:53.549 --> 00:03:56.060
That would be absolutely amazing.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.689
If you have a closer look at the paper, you
will see that it tries to minimize 7 things

00:04:00.689 --> 00:04:02.370
at the same time.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.209
What a time to be alive!

